There are hundreds of hosting providers and they all claim to be fast, reliable, and secure. Here's how to cut through the noise and focus on what actually matters.

## Uptime guarantee

The most fundamental requirement: your website needs to be online. Look for a provider that guarantees 99.9% uptime or above. Anything lower means more downtime than is acceptable for a business website.

Be cautious of guarantees with no stated compensation if they're breached — they're often marketing rather than meaningful commitments.

## Speed and infrastructure

Ask (or check) whether the hosting includes a CDN. A Content Delivery Network significantly improves loading speed by serving your site from servers close to each visitor. Without one, you're depending entirely on the speed of a single server location.

Check reviews specifically mentioning speed and performance — not just overall ratings, which can be skewed.

Read more: [What is a CDN and why does it make your website faster?](/blog/what-is-a-cdn-and-why-does-it-make-your-website-faster/)

## What's included as standard

Before signing up, confirm these are included without extra cost:

- SSL certificate (essential — see [why SSL matters](/blog/what-is-an-ssl-certificate-and-does-your-website-need-one/))
- Daily automatic backups, stored separately from the main server
- Security monitoring or firewall protection
- CDN

If any of these are charged as add-ons, the headline price is misleading.

## Support quality

What happens when something goes wrong? Check what support channels are available (phone, email, live chat), what hours they operate, and how quickly issues are typically resolved. A hosting provider with no human support is a risk for a business that depends on its website.

## Renewal pricing

Many budget hosts offer heavily discounted introductory pricing that doubles or triples on renewal. Check the renewal price, not just the sign-up price, before committing.

## Who manages it

If you're not technically confident, managed hosting — where the provider handles server maintenance, updates, and security — is worth paying for. See our breakdown of [managed hosting vs DIY hosting](/blog/managed-hosting-vs-diy-hosting/) for more detail.

